                        "answer": 0
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "comparatives",
                    "title": "Comparatives",
                    "explanation": "Para comparar usamos el comparativo + than: Valencia is bigger than Bristol.\n\nAdjetivos cortos: + -er (small → smaller, cold → colder). Si acaban en vocal + consonante, la consonante se dobla: big → bigger, wet → wetter. Si acaban en -y: sunny → sunnier, funny → funnier, friendly → friendlier.\n\nAdjetivos largos: more / less + adjetivo: more expensive, more interesting.\n\nIrregulares: good → better, bad → worse, far → further.\n\nPara reforzar: much bigger, much more expensive. Para decir que no son iguales: Bristol isn't as sunny as Valencia.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Adjetivo",
                        "Comparativo",
                        "Regla"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "small",
                          "smaller",
                          "+ -er"
                        ],
                        [
                          "big",
                          "bigger",
                          "dobla la consonante"
                        ],
                        [
                          "sunny",
                          "sunnier",
                          "-y → -ier"
                        ],
                        [
                          "expensive",
                          "more expensive",
                          "more + adjetivo largo"
                        ],
                        [
                          "good",
                          "better",
                          "irregular"
                        ],
                        [
                          "bad",
                          "worse",
                          "irregular"
                        ],
                        [
                          "far",
                          "further",
                          "irregular"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Valencia is bigger and sunnier.",
                      "Bristol is much more expensive.",
                      "The weather is worse here.",
                      "Bristol isn't as grey as people say."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "ed_ing",
                    "title": "Adjectives with -ed and -ing: bored or boring?",
                    "explanation": "Muchos adjetivos tienen dos formas:\n\nCon -ing describen la cosa o la persona que produce el sentimiento: Bristol is interesting (Bristol es interesante). The film was boring (la película era aburrida).\n\nCon -ed describen cómo se siente alguien: I'm interested in art (me interesa el arte). I was bored (estaba aburrido).\n\nOjo con el error típico: «I'm boring» significa «soy aburrido», no «estoy aburrido» (I'm bored).\n\nLos más comunes: interesting / interested, boring / bored, tiring / tired, exciting / excited, surprising / surprised, relaxing / relaxed, worrying / worried.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "-ing (cómo es algo)",
                        "-ed (cómo me siento)"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "The city is interesting.",
                          "I'm interested in the city."
                        ],
                        [
                          "The weather is boring.",
                          "I'm bored."
                        ],
                        [
                          "The trip was tiring.",
                          "We were tired."
                        ],
                        [
                          "The news is exciting.",
                          "Javi is excited."
                        ],
                        [
                          "The price was surprising.",
                          "I was surprised."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Bristol is more interesting than I thought.",
                      "I'm very interested in art and music.",
                      "The bus journey to Bath was really boring.",
                      "After the flight, Lucía was very tired."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "comparatives",
                    "title": "the same as, similar to, different from, like",
                    "explanation": "Para comparar sin -er ni more:\n\n• the same as (igual que): Lucía's flat is the same as Priya's. The same + nombre + as: She's the same age as me.\n• similar to (parecido a): Bristol is similar to Valencia in some ways.\n• different from (diferente de): The food here is very different from the food in Spain. (No «different of».)\n• like (como): Daniel's accent is like my teacher's. Look like = parecerse físicamente: You look like your brother.\n• alike (iguales, parecidos) va al final, sin objeto: Javi and Lucía look alike.\n\nOjo: «igual que» con un verbo es as: She works in design, as I do. Con un nombre o pronombre, like: She's like me.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Expresión",
                        "Ejemplo",
                        "En español"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "the same as",
                          "My phone is the same as yours.",
                          "igual que"
                        ],
                        [
                          "the same + nombre + as",
                          "Sam is the same age as Priya.",
                          "la misma edad que"
                        ],
                        [
                          "similar to",
                          "Bristol is similar to Valencia in some ways.",
                          "parecido a"
                        ],
                        [
                          "different from",
                          "The weather is very different from Spain.",
                          "diferente de"
                        ],
                        [
                          "look like / alike",
                          "Javi looks like his dad. They look alike.",
                          "se parece a / se parecen"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Bristol is very different from Valencia.",
                      "In some ways, Bristol is similar to Madrid: lots of music and art.",
                      "Javi is the same height as Daniel.",
                      "Lucía and Javi look alike. They've got the same eyes."
                    ]
                  }
